          q: "What are the essential golden rules, labeling techniques, and presentation guidelines to score full marks in Class 10 Science diagrams?",
          ans: `<div style="line-height:1.8;">
            <b>Class 10 Science Public Examination: Diagram Presentation Master Guide</b><br><br>
            According to the Tamil Nadu State Board (Samacheer Kalvi) evaluation scheme, science diagrams carry high weightage in 4-mark and 7-mark questions (Part IV: Q.No. 33, 34, 35). Follow these 7 golden rules to guarantee 100% marks in every diagram question:<br><br>
            <b>1. The Sharp HB Pencil Only Rule:</b><br>
            • Draw the outline, internal structures, and pointer lines <b>exclusively with a sharp HB or 2B pencil</b>.<br>
            • <b>Never use black or blue ballpoint/gel pens</b> to draw or write labels inside the diagram area.<br>
            • Avoid heavy, dark shading with lead; clean, single-stroke continuous lines look professional and are easy to correct with a clean eraser.<br><br>
            <b>2. Ruler-Straight Non-Crossing Pointer Lines:</b><br>
            • Always use a clean transparent 30 cm ruler to draw leader/pointer lines from the organ part to the label text.<br>
            • <b>Crucial Rule: Pointer lines must NEVER intersect or cross each other.</b> Crossing lines immediately confuses the evaluator and leads to deduction of marks.<br>
            • Keep pointer lines either strictly horizontal or at a uniform 45° angle ending in a horizontal segment.<br><br>
            <b>3. Unilateral (Right-Side) Label Alignment:</b><br>
            • Whenever feasible, position all label names in a neat vertical column along the <b>right-hand side</b> of the drawing.<br>
            • Write labels in clear, legible print block letters (not cursive handwriting) so medical/botanical terms are unmistakable.<br>
            • Keep labels strictly horizontal; never write text diagonally, vertically, or upside-down.<br><br>
            <b>4. Generous Proportion & Sizing (1/3 to 1/2 Page):</b><br>
            • Draw diagrams adequately large, occupying roughly <b>10 to 14 cm in height (about half of an answer sheet page)</b>.<br>
            • Tiny, cramped diagrams (stamp-sized) make accurate labeling of micro-structures like glomerulus, valves, or nodes of Ranvier impossible and forfeit neatness marks.<br><br>
            <b>5. Compulsory Directional Arrows:</b><br>
            • In <b>Physics Ray Optics</b> (Convex lens, Prism, Myopia/Hypermetropia): Indicating arrow heads on incident, refracted, and emergent rays is <b>legally mandatory</b>. Missing arrows results in 0 marks for the ray diagram even if lines are straight.<br>
            • In <b>Biology Circulation & Nervous Reflexes</b>: Arrows indicating blood flow (Atrium ➔ Ventricle ➔ Aorta) or nerve impulses (Receptor ➔ Sensory ➔ Spinal Cord ➔ Motor ➔ Effector) are strictly required.<br>
            • In <b>Electricity Circuits</b>: Current flow direction (from positive to negative terminal) must be clearly shown.<br><br>
            <b>6. Centered & Underlined Figure Caption:</b><br>
            • Always write a neat, bold title centered beneath the diagram enclosed in a box or underlined (e.g., <u>Fig: Internal Structure of Human Heart</u>).<br><br>
            <b>7. Mark Breakdown in 7-Mark Questions:</b><br>
            • In Part IV (7-Mark questions), diagrams carry <b>4 Marks for drawing and accurate labeling (minimum 4 to 6 correct labels required)</b>.<br>
            • The remaining <b>3 Marks are allotted for written explanation, working principle, or physiological functions</b>.<br>
            • Even if you are running out of time, drawing the diagram and labeling 4 parts will secure 4 out of 7 marks instantly!</div>`
